# Answer 11 · Approaches to Women's Development: WID, WAD and GAD (CSS 2021)
@py question 2021-7

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note A near repeat of 2016: re-cut the material around "approaches to women's development" — place WID, WAD and GAD in the wider sequence of policy approaches, and analyse each critically with Pakistani examples.
@ask CRITICALLY ANALYSE the various APPROACHES to WOMEN'S DEVELOPMENT
@ask focusing on WID, WAD and GAD
(1) Introduction: approaches differ on what women's problem is and how to solve it
(2) Before WID: the welfare approach treated women only as mothers
(3) WID, the idea: integrate women; equity, anti-poverty and efficiency variants
(4) WID, analysis: visibility and resources won; the division of labour left intact
(5) WAD, the idea: women already integrated, but exploited by class and global structures
(6) WAD, analysis: exposed global inequality; ignored patriarchy inside classes
(7) GAD, the idea: transform gender power relations; empowerment and mainstreaming
(8) GAD, analysis: the most complete framework; hard to implement and often depoliticised
(9) The practical–strategic test: which needs each approach meets
(10) Pakistan: from vocational centres to BISP, quotas and property rights
(11) Conclusion: a movement from welfare to empowerment, still incomplete
:::

## Decoding the question
This is a **near repeat** of 2016 Q8, but the wording differs: "**approaches to women's development**". Use this to widen the frame — show that WID, WAD and GAD belong to a **sequence of policy approaches** (welfare, equity, anti-poverty, efficiency, empowerment). **"Critically analyse"** needs strengths **and** weaknesses of each, a **test** for comparing them, and Pakistani examples.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
An **approach to women's development** is a set of assumptions about **what women's problem is** in development and **how policy should solve it**. Since 1950, approaches have moved from seeing women as **mothers** to seeing them as **producers**, then as **exploited workers**, and finally as **agents** whose power relations with men must change.

#### ii) The argument
**WID**, **WAD** and **GAD** mark three stages in this movement. Each corrected the blind spot of the last: WID brought women **in**, WAD exposed the **unequal system**, and GAD focused on **gender power**. Critical analysis shows that each has strengths, and that the most ambitious — GAD — is also the hardest to put into practice.

### 2. Before WID: the welfare approach
From the 1950s, development agencies treated women as **mothers and homemakers**. Programmes offered **food aid, nutrition education, maternal and child health and family planning**. Women were **passive recipients**; their work as farmers and traders was ignored. **Caroline Moser** (==1993==) called this the **welfare approach**. It met some practical needs but reinforced women's domestic role. Women's work as farmers, traders and craftworkers was invisible, and assistance often reached them only through their husbands.

### 3. WID — the idea

#### i) Origins
**Ester Boserup** (*Woman's Role in Economic Development*, ==1970==) showed that modernisation had marginalised women producers. Women professionals in **Washington** coined "Women in Development", and the **Percy Amendment** (==1973==) required US aid to integrate women.

#### ii) Three variants
Moser identified three WID approaches. **Equity** — the original WID — sought equal rights and status through law and access; governments found it threatening. **Anti-poverty** focused on raising poor women's **income** through small projects. **Efficiency**, from the 1980s, involved women because it made development **more efficient** — often by using their unpaid time during structural adjustment.

### 4. WID — critical analysis

#### i) Strengths
WID made women **visible** in development statistics, created **women's ministries** and UN bodies, and won education, credit and training for women. It recognised women as **producers**.

#### ii) Weaknesses
WID accepted the existing model of development — "**add women and stir**". It **took the sexual division of labour for granted**, so women's paid work was added to their unpaid work. It focused on women alone, not on **men's power**, and ignored class and global inequality. Its projects — sewing, handicrafts, poultry — were small and marginal, and its efficiency variant often used women's unpaid time to deliver services cheaply during structural adjustment.

### 5. WAD — the idea

#### i) Origins
In the late 1970s, scholars influenced by **Marxism** and **dependency theory** criticised WID. The IDS workshop on "**The Continuing Subordination of Women**" (==1978==) was a landmark.

#### ii) Principles
WAD argued that women had **always been part of development** — as farmers, workers and carers — but on **unequal and exploitative** terms. The problem was the **nature of development**: an unequal international and class system that exploits both poor men and poor women.

### 6. WAD — critical analysis

#### i) Strengths
WAD asked "**development for whom?**", gave Southern scholars a voice, linked women's position to **class, debt and global trade**, and valued women's existing work.

#### ii) Weaknesses
WAD emphasised **class** and neglected **patriarchy within classes** — why poor men also dominate poor women. It paid little attention to the household and, in practice, often funded separate women's projects again. Its call for changing the international economic order was hard for governments and donors to adopt.

### 7. GAD — the idea

#### i) Origins
GAD emerged in the 1980s from socialist feminists — such as **Kate Young** — and Southern networks such as **DAWN** (==1984==). **Beijing** (==1995==) adopted its language.

#### ii) Principles
GAD focuses on **gender relations** — power between women and men — in households, markets and states. It values both **productive and reproductive** work, treats women as **agents**, and seeks to **transform** unequal structures through **empowerment** and **gender mainstreaming**.

#### iii) Tools
**Practical and strategic needs** (**Molyneux**, ==1985==; **Moser**, ==1993==); Moser's **triple role**; **Naila Kabeer**'s definition of **empowerment** (==1999==) as the ability to make **strategic life choices**.

### 8. GAD — critical analysis

#### i) Strengths
GAD brought **men, power and the household** into analysis, and links everyday needs to long-term change. It treats women as actors rather than beneficiaries, and supports their collective organisation — women's groups, unions and movements — as the engine of change.

#### ii) Weaknesses
Transforming power is political, so agencies often reduce GAD to **checklists and workshops**; "gender" can divert resources from women; **mainstreaming** can leave no one responsible; and men and elites resist.

::: fig spectrum | Approaches to women's development, from meeting roles to changing power.
@left Accept existing gender roles
@right Transform gender relations
8: Welfare — women as mothers
28: WID (anti-poverty, efficiency) — women as producers
45: WID (equity) — equal rights and access
62: WAD — change the unequal system
90: GAD (empowerment) — change power between women and men
:::

### 9. The practical–strategic test
A useful test is which **needs** each approach meets. Welfare and most WID projects meet **practical** needs — food, health, income — without changing women's position. WAD addresses **structural** inequality but mainly between classes and nations. GAD aims to meet practical needs in ways that serve **strategic** interests — land, laws, power. By this test, GAD is the most complete approach, but the one least often fully delivered.

### 10. The Pakistan angle

#### i) Welfare and WID
Pakistan's early programmes were welfare-oriented — maternal and child health, family planning. The **Women's Division** (==1979==), vocational centres, sewing and handicraft training and the **First Women Bank** (==1989==) followed WID.

#### ii) GAD
Later steps reflect GAD: **gender mainstreaming** and budgeting pilots; the **10% quota** for women in federal jobs; **60** reserved seats (==2002==); **BISP** (==2008==) paying cash to women; and the **Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act 2020**. Yet women's labour-force participation remains ==24.4%== and Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026, showing that strategic change is incomplete.

### 11. Conclusion
The approaches to women's development moved from **welfare** (women as mothers), to **WID** (women as producers to be integrated), to **WAD** (women as exploited workers in an unequal system), to **GAD** (gender relations to be transformed). Each advance corrected a blind spot. Critical analysis shows that the problem today is less one of **ideas** than of **implementation** and political will: meeting women's practical needs in ways that genuinely shift power.

## Why this answer scores
- Re-cuts a repeated topic around the question's own words — "**approaches**" — by adding **welfare** and Moser's variants.
- Each approach gets its **idea** and a **critical analysis** (strengths and weaknesses).
- A **spectrum figure** and a **practical–strategic test** show analytical depth.
- Pakistan's programmes are mapped onto the approaches.
